# .privacy-scope — the paths the full-history audit reads. COMMITTED.
#
# Copy to `.privacy-scope` only if this repository needs it. Most do not:
# absent this file the audit reads everything, which is the right behaviour for
# a repository of its own code, and installing the guard narrows nothing.
#
# It exists for a shape the value-based `.privacy-allow` cannot express. A fork
# that vendors a large upstream carries that upstream's files, and with them
# every contact address its maintainers wrote into their own source. Silencing
# those by value would mean committing a list of other people's contact
# details, which is a worse outcome than the finding it answers — so such a
# repository can never come back clean, and a check that cannot reach zero
# stops being read.
#
# WHAT MAKES NARROWING THE AUDIT SAFE is a separate control, and it is the whole
# argument: THE COMMIT HOOK IS NOT SCOPED. It reads every staged line in every
# path, so an identifier written into an excluded directory is still refused at
# the moment someone tries to commit it. The audit is the reporting control and
# may be narrowed; the hook is the preventive one and is not.
#
# One git pathspec per line; `#` starts a comment. Entries may name the paths a
# project writes or exclude the ones it merely carries — for a fork the second
# is usually the practical form, since listing everything an upstream fork
# authors is impractical while naming the vendored directory is one line.
#
# Say WHY beside each entry. An unexplained exclusion is indistinguishable from
# a place someone hid something.
#
# Example — a fork whose vendored packaging carries upstream maintainers'
# addresses, and nothing of the project's own:
#
#   :(exclude)pkgs/pkgbuilds/
#
# Everything not named here stays in scope, which is the point: the files the
# project actually writes are the ones where an identifier would be its own.
